1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:21,000
HOST: In 1519, Hernán Cortés[/es], a conquistador, a Spanish conqueror, landed on the coast of Mexico with five hundred soldiers. Sixteen horses. Fourteen cannons. The Mexica[/es] Empire ruled millions of people from Tenochtitlan[/es], a city larger than London.  Two years later, the empire was gone. Five hundred men did that. How?

2
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:42,400
HOST: By the end of this lesson, you will be able to do three things. One: explain why Spanish weapons alone cannot explain the fall of Tenochtitlan[/es]. Two: describe how smallpox and the Tlaxcalan[/es] alliance decided the conquest. Three: compare the falls of the Mexica[/es] and Inca empires as one repeating pattern.

3
00:00:42,400 --> 00:01:02,400
HOST: The obvious answer is the weapons. Steel swords, steel armor, horses, guns. And in open battle, it was real.  At Otumba[/es], a few dozen Spanish horsemen charged again and again, aiming straight for the Mexica[/es] commanders, and broke an entire army.

4
00:01:02,400 --> 00:01:28,200
CORTES: They had never seen a horse. Never heard a gun. We were few, but we were thunder.
HOST: So guns win?  Not so fast. Cortés[/es] had fourteen cannons. Fourteen. They took a minute to reload. And the Mexica[/es] adapted. They stopped meeting the cavalry in open fields and fought where horses couldn't go. The weapons helped. But fourteen cannons don't topple an empire.

5
00:01:28,200 --> 00:01:53,600
HOST: Here's what the cannons can't explain. In the fall of 1520, smallpox tore through Tenochtitlan[/es]. It killed the emperor Cuitláhuac[/es] after eighty days on the throne. It killed warriors, priests, leaders, everyone who could organize a defense.  A Franciscan friar named Sahagún[/es] spent decades interviewing Mexica[/es] survivors. He asked the survivors what the sickness was like. One of them told him.

6
00:01:53,600 --> 00:02:11,000
WITNESS: The sores covered us, head to foot. We could not walk, could not move. We lay starving in our houses. There was no one left to care for us, no one left to bury the dead, no one left to fight.

7
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:49,199
HOST: Cortés[/es] already knew the trick. Before the plague, before the siege, he'd walked into Tenochtitlan[/es] and seized Moctezuma[/es] himself, holding the emperor hostage for months. And he didn't come back alone. The Tlaxcalans[/es], the Mexica[/es]'s oldest enemies, gave him tens of thousands of warriors. Why fight for the strangers? The Mexica[/es] had spent generations raiding Tlaxcala[/es] for tribute and captives for sacrifice, the Flower Wars. The Tlaxcalans[/es] didn't join Cortés[/es]. They used him. At the final siege, the Spanish gave the orders. The Tlaxcalans[/es] did the fighting and took most of the casualties.

8
00:02:49,199 --> 00:03:21,399
HOST: In Peru, eleven years later, Francisco Pizarro[/es] ran the same play on an empire already broken. Smallpox had killed the Inca emperor Huayna Capac[/es] around 1527, years before Pizarro[/es] arrived. The disease outran him. Huayna Capac[/es]'s sons, Huáscar[/es] and Atahualpa[/es], then fought a brutal war of succession, brother against brother. Atahualpa[/es] won, but the empire was bleeding when Pizarro[/es] walked in.  At Cajamarca[/es], in 1532, Pizarro[/es]'s hundred and sixty-eight men seized the emperor Atahualpa[/es] in the town square.

9
00:03:21,399 --> 00:03:32,799
HOST: They ransomed him for a room full of gold, then executed him anyway. An empire built on one irreplaceable head can't organize a defense without it.

10
00:03:32,799 --> 00:03:52,999
HOST: So here's the playbook. Turn the empire's enemies into your army. Cut off the head. Let disease do the rest.  Guns helped, but they were the smallest part of the answer. [2s pause] So who conquered Mexico? Cortés[/es] gave the orders. [2s pause] Everyone else did the conquering.

11
00:03:52,999 --> 00:04:02,999
